# Independent review of the C256 async B ring

The C++ draft has no source-level correctness blocker. This review is CPU-only: it does not establish compiled scheduling, numerical parity, sanitizer cleanliness, residency, or speed. The candidate remains private and unmeasured.

The independent executable audit is [window_cp_async_b_independent_review.py.draft](../tools/window_cp_async_b_independent_review.py.draft); its [JSON result](../outputs/window_cp_async_b_independent_review.json.draft) pins the candidate, baseline, original NCU metrics, local CUDA occupancy header and audit by SHA256.

The ring occupies bytes 16,384–40,959 of the shared object. The unchanged A exchange occupies bytes 0–16,383. Each warp/lane owns two 16-byte vectors in each of three ring slots. All 24,576 ring bytes have exactly one such owner, and every global and shared vector is aligned and bounded. Enumeration covers 36,864 vector copies across all nine dense roles. The staged x/y/z/w words match the existing cache loads, and all 294,912 lane-level dense MMA events retain the original A operands, B operands and ascending K order for each accumulator.

Each K commits its own pair of copies. K0 and K1 are preissued; each iteration may issue K+2, waits for the required current group, reads its own two vectors, and executes the same 16 MMAs. Slots overwritten by K+2 were already consumed in the preceding iteration. The tail waits for one remaining future group at K6 and drains all groups at K7. That final drain makes reuse in the next dense call safe even when different warps advance at different speeds. No lane reads another lane's ring allocation.

This matches NVIDIA's documented per-thread completion and visibility rules for [`cp.async.wait_group`](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/#data-movement-and-conversion-instructions-cp-async-wait-group). The copy and wait inline assembly includes memory clobbers; all seven existing CTA barriers and the surrounding kernel arithmetic are unchanged. Negative models deliberately merge the initial groups, reuse a two-slot ring too early, or omit the draining tail; each is rejected.

The resource assumptions agree with the saved SM120 report and the installed CUDA 13.4 `include/cuda_occupancy.h`:

| Resource | Saved baseline or candidate capacity calculation |
| --- | ---: |
| SM shared-memory maximum | 102,400 bytes |
| Default per-block shared limit | 49,152 bytes |
| Observed driver reservation per block | 1,024 bytes |
| Candidate static allocation | 40,960 bytes |
| Candidate total with that reservation | 41,984 bytes |
| Baseline actual/allocated registers per thread | 162 / 168 |
| Baseline block size | 256 threads |
| Register file per SM | 65,536 registers |

The local occupancy code includes SM120 carveouts of 0/8/16/32/64/100 KiB and rounds an insufficient preferred allocation up to a supported size. Thus this draft can use its static allocation without a per-launch attribute mutation. The actual driver choice still needs to be recorded. NVIDIA also documents [carveout rounding and driver discretion](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/03-advanced/advanced-kernel-programming.html#configuring-l1-shared-memory-balance).

A 64-KiB carveout fits one 41-KiB block. A 100-KiB carveout can fit two by shared capacity, but the observed 168 allocated registers per thread permit only one 256-thread block. Even 136 allocated registers still permit only one; two would require at most 128 allocated registers per thread as well as sufficient shared carveout. These are capacity bounds, not a prediction of the candidate's register allocation or a request to force a larger carveout.

Before interpreting timing, inspect actual SASS for retained async requests, correct commit/wait placement, and the intended request lead. Also inspect the B shared loads: vector `LDS.128` can service the consecutive 16-byte lanes in 128-byte transactions, whereas separately issued scalar loads with a 16-byte lane stride can introduce bank conflicts. Source `uint4` syntax does not prove how the compiler lowers them. Register count, local spills, configured shared capacity and eligible warps remain measured questions. A latency reduction could still be offset by waits, additional shared traffic or address work.

This audit changed only new review drafts. It did not alter the candidate, active code, policies, binary or profiling reports.
